// @no-instantiate expected type i32
// @hint redundant type annotation: `ys` is inferred as `string[]` — remove it to lean on inference
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. An array literal whose FIRST element is a literal-union atom builds
// an ATOM list and ignores the `string[]` annotation on the binding. `vl check --codegen` exits
// 0 and the module does not validate.
//
// NOT A MISSING WIDEN, which is what distinguishes it from its sibling
// (tests/cases/literal-unions/litunion-atom-in-string-array-literal.vl, where a string-FIRST
// literal builds a string list and the atom element simply needed converting). Here the wrong
// CONTAINER is built: `arrLitIsStr` declines on `nodeArrayElemIsLitUnion`, so the emitter
// produces a list whose backing is `(array (mut i32))` while the binding's slot is backed by
// `(array (mut (ref null 1)))`.
//
// ELEMENT ORDER IS THE ONLY DIFFERENCE between the two files, which is what makes this worth
// pinning separately rather than folding into the widen:
//
//   const ys: string[] = ["plain", v]   // string list built, atom element widens — WORKS
//   const ys: string[] = [v, "plain"]   // atom list built, string element does not fit — HERE
//   const ys: string[] = [v]            // atom list built, annotation ignored — HERE
//
// A `K[]` annotation over the same literal is correct and is the control: that genuinely IS the
// atom list. So the defect is that the ANNOTATION loses to the element type, not that either
// list rep is wrong.
//
// THE CHECKER IS RIGHT AND THE EMITTER IS WRONG, which the `@hint` proves rather than argues:
// the linter reports `ys` is inferred as `string[]`, so the checker DID resolve the literal to a
// string array. The emitter then built an atom list anyway. This is a checker/codegen split of
// the same shape as `xfail-miscompile-local-shadowing-param-ignored.vl`, not a checker bug and
// not an inference gap — which narrows the fix to `arrLitIsStr`'s consultation of
// `nodeArrayElemIsLitUnion` rather than to context propagation, as an earlier draft of this
// header guessed.
//
// Fixing it means making `arrLitIsStr` prefer the BINDING's element type over the literal's own
// when they disagree — the emitter asking the same question the checker already answered. That
// is a change to a classifier every array literal in the language flows through, which is why it
// is pinned here rather than folded into the sibling's one-line element widen.
type K = "ka" | "kb"
function main() {
  const v: K = "kb"
  const ys: string[] = [v, "plain"]
  print(ys[1])
}
main()
